.TH FL-WM 1 "November 2026" "fldesktop 0.1.0" "fldesktop"
.SH NAME
fl-wm \- Wayland compositor of the fldesktop desktop
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBfl-wm\fR [\fB\-c\fR \fIFILE\fR] [\fB\-s\fR \fICMD\fR] [\fB\-n\fR] [\fB\-d\fR] [\fB\-v\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBfl-wm\fR is a stacking Wayland compositor based on wlroots. It draws server side
window decorations, handles key bindings, Alt+Tab switching and window snapping, and
pins the fldesktop shell clients (fl-panel, fl-desktop, fl-launcher) to their places.
It runs on a VT (DRM/KMS) or nested inside another Wayland or X11 session.
.SH WINDOW SWITCHER
Alt+Tab (and Alt+Shift+Tab) opens a panel with the open windows, most recently used first, each
with its application icon and title; minimized windows are shown faded. While Alt is held, Tab,
Shift+Tab and the arrow keys move the selection; releasing Alt, Enter or a click switches to the
window (restoring it when minimized), Escape or a click outside closes the panel. With
\fBswitcher=false\fR in the \fB[behaviour]\fR section of \fIwm.ini\fR each Alt+Tab raises the next
window directly instead. Application icons come from the windows' desktop files and the icon theme
(SVG icons need librsvg).
.SH WORKSPACES
There are four workspaces by default (\fBworkspaces=\fR1 to 9 in the \fB[behaviour]\fR section of
\fIwm.ini\fR); all outputs switch together. Super+1 to Super+9 go to a workspace and
Super+Ctrl+Left/Right to the previous or next one; Super+Shift+1 to Super+Shift+9 send the focused
window to a workspace, Super+Ctrl+Shift+Left/Right take it along to the previous or next one. The
\fBsticky\fR action (unbound by default) shows a window on every workspace. New windows open on the
current workspace. The panel's window list and Alt+Tab show the windows of the current workspace;
activating a window of another workspace (a notification, another program asking for it) goes
there. With wlroots 0.20 the workspaces are published through ext-workspace-v1, which the panel's
pager uses.
.SH ACCESSIBILITY
\fBSuper+=\fR (or Super+Plus) zooms the screen in around the pointer in steps from 1.5x to
8x, \fBSuper+Minus\fR zooms out and \fBSuper+0\fR ends the zoom (actions \fBzoom-in\fR,
\fBzoom-out\fR, \fBzoom-reset\fR). The point under the pointer stays where it is, so
what the pointer shows is what it clicks.
.PP
In the \fB[accessibility]\fR section of \fIwm.ini\fR, \fBsticky_keys=true\fR makes a
modifier (Shift, Ctrl, Alt, Super) pressed and released on its own apply to the next key, so
shortcuts can be typed one key at a time; pressed twice it stays on until pressed once more.
\fBbounce_keys=\fR\fIMS\fR ignores a key pressed again within \fIMS\fR milliseconds of its
release.
.SH X11 PROGRAMS
X11 programs (Steam, games, Wine, older Electron and Java programs) run through
\fBXwayland\fR, which fl-wm starts when the first one connects; \fBDISPLAY\fR is
set for the programs fl-wm starts. Their windows get the same decorations,
taskbar entries, Alt+Tab, snapping and maximizing as Wayland windows; windows
that ask for no decorations (Motif hints) get none, and windows with a position
hint keep their position. Menus and tooltips (override-redirect windows) are
shown above the windows as the program places them. \fBxwayland=false\fR in the
\fB[behaviour]\fR section of \fIwm.ini\fR turns it off (read at startup only).
It needs wlroots built with Xwayland support and the \fBXwayland\fR program.
.SH SCREEN LOCKING
SIGUSR1, the \fBlock\fR key binding (Super+L) and the idle timeout
([lock] idle_minutes) lock the session: fl-wm covers all outputs, gives the
keyboard only to the locker and starts \fBfl-lock \-\-locker\fR (see
\fBfl-lock\fR(1)). It unlocks when that process exits with status 0 and
restarts it if it dies otherwise. Clients of the ext-session-lock-v1
protocol, such as swaylock, can lock the session as well.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
The [environment] section of \fIwm.ini\fR sets variables for the programs
started from then on (it is applied at startup and on every reload):
\fBNAME=value\fR sets, \fBNAME=\fR unsets and \fBNAME=@inherit\fR
restores the value fl-wm started with. Settings > Proxy writes the proxy
variables here.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-c, \-\-config FILE\fR
Read FILE instead of ~/.config/fldesktop/wm.ini.
.TP
\fB\-s, \-\-startup CMD\fR
Run CMD after startup instead of the [autostart] programs.
.TP
\fB\-n, \-\-no\-autostart\fR
Do not run the [autostart] programs.
.TP
\fB\-d, \-\-debug\fR
Verbose logging.
.TP
\fB\-v, \-\-version\fR
Print the version.
.SH FILES
\fI~/.config/fldesktop/wm.ini\fR, falling back to \fI/etc/xdg/fldesktop/wm.ini\fR.
An annotated example is installed as \fI/usr/share/fldesktop/wm.ini.example\fR.
SIGHUP or Super+Shift+R reloads the configuration; SIGUSR1 locks the session.
.SH SEE ALSO
\fBfl-session\fR(1), \fBfl-panel\fR(1), \fBfl-lock\fR(1)
